# src/aerocanyon/ardupilot/tricopter.parm
# QuadPlane tilt-tricopter -- see docs/superpowers/specs/2026-09-02-tricopter-ardupilot-phase1-design.md
# for the geometry this encodes (front -20..90deg incl. hover-yaw-trim
# sub-range + stop/fold in cruise, rear 90..180deg always-on pusher).
#
# Every param name below was confirmed against the ~/ardupilot source tree
# for this checkout (ArduPlane/quadplane.cpp, ArduPlane/tiltrotor.cpp,
# libraries/AC_AttitudeControl/AC_AttitudeControl_Multi.cpp,
# libraries/AP_Arming/AP_Arming.cpp, libraries/SITL/SITL.cpp,
# libraries/AP_SerialManager/AP_SerialManager.cpp) -- none are guessed.

# --- Frame / tilt geometry -- exact values from Task 2's live SITL spike ---
Q_ENABLE          1
# Q_FRAME_CLASS changed 7->17 (Tri -> Dynamic Scripting Matrix) 2026-09-04
# -- see docs/superpowers/specs/2026-09-04-scripting-mixer-fw-transition-design.md
# and the matching plan. Q_FRAME_TYPE/Q_TILT_* below are UNCHANGED and
# still apply to the front pair's own tilt-yaw mechanism; only the
# overall mixer class and the rear motor's role change.
Q_FRAME_CLASS     17       # Dynamic Scripting Matrix -- see scripts/tricopter_mixer.lua
Q_FRAME_TYPE      1
Q_TILT_ENABLE     1
Q_TILT_MASK       3        # front pair (motors 1+2) ONLY -- rear stays out, see Task 2 section 5
Q_TILT_TYPE       2        # VectoredYaw -- required for the front pair's own tilt-yaw mechanism
Q_TILT_YAW_ANGLE  20       # front pair's -20..0 deg hover yaw-trim sub-range
Q_TILT_FIX_ANGLE  0        # must stay 0, or it eats into the range maths
Q_TILT_MAX        20        # degrees -- raised from 8 (itself down from 45) after
# live-testing on the zeroed-altitude map_zone world: 8 deg was too
# conservative and capped forward cruise speed to ~1.4 m/s even with
# Q_WP_SPD=10. 20 deg gives a reasonable ~5 m/s cruise while staying well
# under the lift-destroying territory near 45 -- see the AUTO-mission
# note further down for why this fallback exists at all.
# RATE_UP/RATE_DN are named for forward-to-hover / hover-to-forward
# respectively (ArduPlane/tiltrotor.cpp's own @Description, opposite of
# what the names suggest). At the old 40 deg/s, the full Q_TILT_MAX=8deg
# sweep completed in ~0.2s -- effectively an instant step at arm time,
# not a settle. Slowed to 15 deg/s (still well inside the documented
# 10-300 range) for a smoother ~0.5s tilt-servo transition instead of a
# snap; explicitly set both rather than leaving RATE_DN at its
# fall-back-to-RATE_UP default of 0, so the two directions can be tuned
# independently later if needed.
Q_TILT_RATE_UP    15
Q_TILT_RATE_DN    15

# Motor thrust channels -- must match Task 3's Gazebo ArduPilotPlugin channels
# 4/5/7 (0-indexed) = SERVO5/6/8 (1-indexed). Physical wiring (which
# SERVO channel drives which Gazebo motor joint) is fixed and did NOT
# change with the 2026-09-04 Q_FRAME_CLASS=17 switch -- SERVO5 is still
# front-right, SERVO6 still front-left, SERVO8 still rear.
#
# What DID change: AP_MotorsTri (old Q_FRAME_CLASS=7) used its own
# special motor1/motor2/motor4 numbering (skipping motor3). The generic
# AP_MotorsMatrix-based Dynamic Scripting Matrix (new Q_FRAME_CLASS=17)
# instead expects motor index N (as passed to
# scripts/tricopter_mixer.lua's Motors_dynamic:add_motor(N, ...)) to map
# straight to k_motor(N+1) -- motor 0->k_motor1, 1->k_motor2, 2->k_motor3,
# no skipping. The script registers 0=front-right, 1=rear, 2=front-left,
# so SERVO8 (rear) needs k_motor2 now, not k_motor4, and SERVO6
# (front-left) needs k_motor3, not k_motor2. Live-caught 2026-09-04: with
# the old assignments still in place, front-left got no motor command at
# all (SERVO8_raw read 0 on arming) since nothing was assigned k_motor3.
SERVO5_FUNCTION   33       # k_motor1 -> script motor index 0 -> front right
SERVO6_FUNCTION   35       # k_motor3 -> script motor index 2 -> front left
SERVO8_FUNCTION   34       # k_motor2 -> script motor index 1 -> rear
SERVO7_FUNCTION   0
SERVO3_FUNCTION   0        # no separate fixed fwd throttle in Phase 1 (see Task 2 section 5 cruise-thrust concern)

# Tilt servo channels -- must match Task 3's Gazebo ArduPilotPlugin channels
# 11/12/13 (0-indexed) = SERVO12/13/14 (1-indexed).
# PWM->angle mapping verified live in Task 2 (SITL, cold-reboot persistence checked):
#   front (SERVO12/13): PWM 1000..2000 -> -20deg..+90deg
#   rear  (SERVO14):    PWM 1000..2000 -> +90deg..+200deg (REVERSED=1 required;
#                        SERVOn_MIN>MAX does NOT invert, per SRV_Channel::pwm_from_range's guard)
SERVO12_FUNCTION  76       # k_tiltMotorRight -> front right tilt
SERVO12_MIN       1000
SERVO12_MAX       2000
SERVO12_REVERSED  0

SERVO13_FUNCTION  75       # k_tiltMotorLeft -> front left tilt
SERVO13_MIN       1000
SERVO13_MAX       2000
SERVO13_REVERSED  0

# SERVO14_FUNCTION changed 45->94 (k_tiltMotorRear -> k_scripting1)
# 2026-09-04 -- scripts/tricopter_mixer.lua now owns the rear tilt
# schedule directly (in lockstep with its own motor-factor-table swap),
# not ArduPilot's own Tiltrotor class. The script's own scaled-output
# convention (0=down/hover, 1000=horizontal/cruise) is designed to match
# the SDF's native motor_1_joint range directly (0=upright, +pi/2=
# forward -- see model.sdf's own comment on that joint), the SAME
# convention the UNREVERSED front tilt servos (SERVO12/13 below) use --
# not the old k_tiltMotorRear+REVERSED=1 180deg-offset workaround this
# replaces. REVERSED changed 1->0 to match. NEEDS LIVE VERIFICATION
# (see the matching plan's Task 2/3) -- if the rear servo ends up moving
# the wrong direction, flip this back to 1 first before suspecting the
# script logic.
SERVO14_FUNCTION  94
SERVO14_MIN       1200
SERVO14_TRIM      1500
SERVO14_MAX       1900
SERVO14_REVERSED  0

# --- Rate gains: starting point ONLY, reasoned from this session's PX4 ---
# empirical tuning (MC_ROLL_P=5.0, MC_ROLLRATE_P/I/D=0.2/0.2/0.004,
# MC_YAWRATE_P/I/D=0.16/0.027/0.004, hover thrust fraction ~0.7).
# ArduPilot's rate controller scales gains differently than PX4's
# allocator-mediated ones (different units, different filtering, different
# feedforward structure) -- these are a REASONED GUESS to start iterating
# from in Task 5, NOT a port. Pitch mirrors roll (symmetric airframe in
# pitch); PX4's MC_ROLLRATE_* had no direct pitch equivalent recorded this
# session, so pitch is set equal to roll rather than invented separately.
Q_A_RAT_RLL_P     0.20
Q_A_RAT_RLL_I     0.20
Q_A_RAT_RLL_D     0.004
Q_A_RAT_PIT_P     0.20
Q_A_RAT_PIT_I     0.20
Q_A_RAT_PIT_D     0.004
Q_A_RAT_YAW_P     0.25       # up from 0.16 -- Q_TILT_MAX=20's wider forward-tilt
# swing on the front pair now visibly fights the same servos' yaw-trim
# duty (Q_TILT_TYPE=2 VectoredYaw shares them). Raising P/D pushes the
# yaw-rate loop through that disturbance harder; live-verify for
# oscillation before trusting this over more than one flight.
Q_A_RAT_YAW_I     0.03        # up slightly from 0.027, holds against sustained bias
Q_A_RAT_YAW_D     0.008       # doubled from 0.004, damps the extra P

# --- Q-mode attitude limit (Phase 2 gain-tuning pass) ---
# Q_A_ANGLE_MAX (AC_AttitudeControl's ANGLE_MAX, exposed here under the
# Q_ group; stored in plain DEGREES -- confirmed against
# AC_AttitudeControl.cpp's own @Units: deg and the _angle_max_deg field
# name, not centidegrees) defaults to 30 degrees -- the actual
# lean-angle ceiling full RC stick deflection commands in QHOVER.
# controller_node.py's own lean commands were found (live) to already
# saturate full stick well before this limit even mattered (the desired
# lean from a large position error is tens of degrees, clipped to +-1
# regardless of the Python-side MAX_LEAN_RAD normalization constant) --
# so the achievable cruise speed (~5 m/s observed, against the mission's
# 12 m/s target) is bounded by THIS parameter, not by anything on the
# Python side. Raised modestly, not aggressively -- see
# docs/superpowers/plans/2026-09-02-mission-stack-mavros-port.md for the
# gain-tuning trail.
Q_A_ANGLE_MAX     40       # degrees, up from 35 -- more forward-lean authority for
# AC_PosControl to build cruise speed via pitch (main-rotor thrust
# vectoring) rather than leaning on the Q_TILT_MAX fallback. Hover sits
# at 47% throttle (Q_M_THST_HOVER), leaving ~53% margin for the added
# vertical-thrust loss from the steeper lean.

# --- RC-override failsafe margin (Phase 2, mid-flight-landing bug) ---
# RC_OVERRIDE_TIME (RC_Channel.cpp / RC_Channels_VarInfo.h, confirmed
# against ArduPilot's own Tools/autotest/rover.py usage) is how long an
# RC_CHANNELS_OVERRIDE is honoured after the last one arrives before
# ArduPilot falls back to its own RC failsafe handling -- default 3.0s.
# controller_node.py publishes overrides at 50 Hz continuously, but was
# live-verified this session to occasionally miss that cadence for long
# enough to trip this and self-land mid-mission, well short of the
# canyon exit, with no error anywhere in the stack (root-caused to
# cbf_filter.py calling its scipy optimizer on every tick regardless of
# whether anything was actually constraining the command -- fixed
# separately, see that file's own fast-path comment). Raised as
# defense-in-depth against any OTHER source of publish jitter (this is
# a simulated vehicle on a shared, loaded machine, not real hardware
# with a dedicated RC link) -- not a substitute for the real fix above.
RC_OVERRIDE_TIME  15       # seconds, up from the 3s default

# --- Hover thrust (Task 5, measured in flight) ---
# The Gazebo model's LiftDrag rig gives ~36.2 N per rotor at full command
# vs 50.7 N of vehicle weight, so hover sits near 47% thrust, not the 35%
# ArduPilot defaults to. Confirmed live: steady hover in QHOVER settles at
# 47-49% throttle. Leaving the default 0.35 makes QHOVER's altitude loop
# start from a bad feedforward on every takeoff.
Q_M_THST_HOVER    0.47

# --- Q-mode waypoint-navigation speed (AUTO-mission redesign) ---
# AC_WPNav's Q_WP_SPD defaults to 10 m/s (WP_SPD_DEFAULT, AC_WPNav.cpp).
# Lowered to 5.0 as a first attempt at the climb-then-crash symptom
# below, kept there even after finding the real cause (Q_TILT_MAX,
# above) since 10 m/s is an aggressive cruise target for this frame's
# thrust margin -- raised again at the user's request back to the
# ArduPilot default.
Q_WP_SPD          10.0       # m/s, ArduPilot's own default

# --- Root cause of the above: Tiltrotor::continuous_update's AUTO-mode
# forward-tilt fallback (Q_TILT_MAX, moved up next to the other tilt
# params) ---
# Live-verified this session: in AUTO (unlike QHOVER, which this
# project's earlier hover tuning was done in), ArduPlane/tiltrotor.cpp's
# continuous_update() does NOT hold the front tilt motors near their
# hover baseline. With Q_FWD_THR_USE left at its OFF default (no
# forward-thrust-motor substitute configured), get_vfwd_method()
# returns NONE, and continuous_update() falls through to its final,
# unconditional branch: settilt = (throttle_output - throttle_min) *
# 0.02, slew(MIN(settilt * Q_TILT_MAX / 90, ~1.0)) -- forward tilt
# driven straight off throttle percentage, with NO mode-specific gating
# and no dependency on Q_VFWD_GAIN/Q_FWD_THR_GAIN actually being
# configured (their descriptions suggest otherwise, but the code path
# taken here doesn't check them). At the climb throttle this frame
# needs (hovers around 47%, Q_M_THST_HOVER above), that formula was
# already commanding tilt near Q_TILT_MAX degrees on the front pair --
# and since only the front pair tilts (Q_TILT_MASK=3, rear stays
# vertical), tilting them forward directly removes vertical thrust with
# nothing compensating for it. Confirmed live: at the old Q_TILT_MAX=45
# this reproduced on every AUTO-mission attempt regardless of Q_WP_SPD
# -- climb to ~10-12m during NAV_VTOL_TAKEOFF, then a rapid descent to
# near-ground once cruise throttle triggered the tilt, settling into a
# low crawl instead of continuing to the next waypoint. This project
# never wants ANY forward-tilt thrust assist (ENABLE_VTOL_TRANSITION
# stays False in controller_node.py -- pure Q-mode navigation the whole
# flight), so rather than wiring up Q_FWD_THR_USE/GAIN correctly for a
# behavior this project doesn't want, Q_TILT_MAX is capped low enough
# that this fallback's worst case is a few degrees of unwanted tilt,
# not a lift-destroying 45. (Q_TILT_YAW_ANGLE's own -20..0 deg hover-
# yaw-trim sub-range is a separate, absolute-degree servo-calibration
# offset applied in Tiltrotor::vectoring() -- confirmed unaffected by
# this change.)

# --- PSC Z (altitude/throttle loop) gains -- the "untried next" item
# from the AUTO-mission tilt bug above. First attempt at smoothing the
# fallback's real remaining trigger: throttle_output ITSELF, still
# capped at 8 deg via Q_TILT_MAX above but reacting on every
# vertical-velocity error via Tiltrotor::continuous_update()'s
# unconditional settilt = (throttle_output - throttle_min) * 0.02.
# Live-observed this session: heading during ordinary AUTO cruise
# doesn't just fail to converge, it wanders unpredictably (e.g. one
# transit leg drifted from a correct ~89 deg heading at AUTO-engage to
# 233 deg fifty seconds later) -- consistent with a twitchy vertical
# loop feeding variable, uncommanded tilt into the SAME front-pair
# servos VectoredYaw (Q_TILT_TYPE=2) uses for yaw trim, fighting it.
# Q_P_D_VEL_P (ArduPilot default 5.0, never tuned for this frame) is
# the most directly-coupled lever: it sets how aggressively a small
# vertical-velocity error becomes a throttle_output swing. Lowered to
# 3.0 as a first, conservative test -- not yet confirmed to fix the
# heading wander, just the first untried change in this direction.
Q_P_D_VEL_P       3.0

FLTMODE_CH        0        # disable RC mode-switch: control_server pins ch8 to 1500,
                           # which ArduPilot would otherwise read as a mode-switch position

# --- Arming / simulation ---
# NOTE: this ArduPilot checkout has already migrated ARMING_CHECK (old
# AP_Int16 "checks to perform" bitmask) to ARMING_SKIPCHK (new AP_Int32
# "checks to SKIP" bitmask, inverted sense) -- see
# libraries/AP_Arming/AP_Arming.cpp:230-260. The brief's plan-era
# `ARMING_CHECK 0` name/value no longer exists in this tree and would be
# silently dropped as an unknown param. -1 (all bits set) is the current
# equivalent of the old "disable all checks" behaviour: check_enabled()
# returns (checks_to_skip & check) == 0, so -1 skips everything.
ARMING_SKIPCHK    -1
SIM_SPEEDUP       1
SERIAL0_PROTOCOL  2
# (The brief also listed a bare `FRAME_CLASS 1` here. No such top-level
# param exists for ArduPlane -- confirmed absent from ArduPlane/Parameters.cpp
# and Parameters.h. Q_FRAME_CLASS above is the real, already-set equivalent.
# Omitted rather than writing an unknown param.)

# --- Waypoint acceptance radius -- live-caught 2026-09-04 ---
# Never overridden here before, so the local SITL was flying on
# ArduPlane's own compiled default of 90m -- a fixed-wing convention
# (planes need room to turn), way too loose for this hovering
# multicopter's waypoint transitions. Corrected live to 5m.
WP_RADIUS         5

# --- Airspeed sensor -- FW-transition prerequisite, 2026-09-04 ---
# Never overridden here before either, so the compiled default
# (ARSPD_TYPE=2, Analog -- a physical-pin sensor, meaningless in
# simulation) was in effect, explaining the recurring "Preflight Fail:
# Airspeed selector module down" this project's history documents on
# both autopilot branches. The Gazebo side was already correctly wired
# -- tricopter_ap/model.sdf includes model://airspeed, and
# map_zone_ap.sdf loads gz-sim-air-speed-system -- and ArduPilot's own
# SIM_JSON.cpp already accepts an `airspeed` field over the JSON-FDM
# link (SIM_JSON.h:148) -- the ONLY missing piece was telling ArduPilot
# to actually read it. ARSPD_TYPE=100 is ArduPilot's own SITL backend
# (reads the simulation's true airspeed directly, not an emulated
# analog/I2C device -- see AP_Airspeed::TYPE_SITL). Without this,
# ArduPlane/quadplane.cpp's transition logic (~line 1394-1518) has no
# ahrs.airspeed_EAS() to wait on and falls back to a groundspeed
# estimate instead (quadplane.cpp:1491) -- close enough in still air,
# but groundspeed != airspeed in any wind, exactly the condition this
# project's wind_field_node tests.
ARSPD_TYPE        100
ARSPD_USE         1

# --- QRTL climb altitude -- live-caught 2026-09-04 ---
# Default 15m was too low for map_zone's real Bucharest building heights,
# risking a collision during the VTOL climb-then-return home. Raised to 50m.
Q_RTL_ALT         50

# --- Dynamic scripting motor mixer -- FW transition, 2026-09-04 ---
# See docs/superpowers/specs/2026-09-04-scripting-mixer-fw-transition-design.md
# for why: Q_FRAME_CLASS=7 (Tri) has no way to give the rear motor an
# independent cruise-only role -- ArduPilot's own tiltrotor.cpp assumes
# the FRONT (tilting) pair becomes the cruise thruster, the opposite of
# this vehicle's real design. Q_FRAME_CLASS=17 (Dynamic Scripting
# Matrix, set above alongside the other Q_FRAME_* params) hands
# motor-factor allocation to scripts/tricopter_mixer.lua instead.
SCR_ENABLE        1

# --- Throttle failsafe -- disabled for SITL bench testing, 2026-09-04 ---
# Live-caught: with no real RC transmitter/joystick source (pure
# MAVLink/scripted testing), ArduPilot correctly sees no valid RC signal
# and raises throttle failsafe (real param name confirmed against
# ArduPlane/Parameters.cpp's GSCALAR(throttle_fs_enabled, "THR_FAILSAFE",
# ...) -- compiled default Enabled), which clamps channel 3 to a
# safe-low PWM regardless of any RC-override command sent over MAVLink
# -- confirmed live via RC_CHANNELS echoing chan3_raw=1000 despite an
# active rc_channels_override_send(...,1900,...). This is correct,
# intentional ArduPilot safety behaviour, not a bug -- disabled here
# only because this project drives the vehicle entirely via
# MAVLink/scripted commands, never a real transmitter. Re-enable (or
# remove this override) before any test involving actual RC hardware.
THR_FAILSAFE      0
